            <ArticleTitle>Myocardial bridging: light in the tunnel.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>555-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10554-009-9470-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Myocardial bridging is a congenital anomaly in which a segment of a coronary artery runs intramuscularly. Although traditionally considered as a benign condition, myocardial bridging may be associated with clinically important complications such as myocardial ischemia, acute coronary syndromes and sudden death. We report the case of a highly symptomatic 36 years old patient with a myocardial bridge in left anterior descending coronary artery in which surgical treatment was proposed. Previous to surgery a non invasive coronariography with cardiac CT was practised in order to define the anatomy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
